  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the \"two-interval symmetric stepfunction\" representation using a more sophisticated optimization algorithm than the previous SLSQP. We replace SLSQP with a Bayesian optimization approach using Tree of Parzen Estimators (TPE) from OptimizeBayes, which is better suited for non-convex problems with few function evaluations. This method explores the space of functions defined by their breakpoints and heights within fixed-duration intervals, rather than just step functions. The key improvements are:\n   - Broader exploration: Consider functions with 2-6 fixed-duration intervals (widths) instead of just two\n   - Adaptive interval scheduling: Start with coarser intervals and refine as the optimization progresses\n   - No gradient required: TPE handles exploration vs exploitation balance properly\n   - Feasibility repair: Automatically scales the function to meet the integral constraint without destroying structure\n   - Bayesian optimization: More efficiently uses function evaluations compared to SLSQP\n   \nThis addresses the bottleneck of local optimization by using a global optimizer that intelligently balances exploration and exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe define piecewise constant functions with fixed-duration intervals at integer start positions. The function is defined by:\n   - Number of intervals (n_segments, between 2 and 6)\n   - Start positions (start_i) for each interval (non-decreasing integers between 0 and n_points-1)\n   - End positions (end_i) for each interval (end_i = start_i + n_half, ensuring the function is symmetric over the interval)\n   - Heights (heights_i) between 0 and 1 for each interval\n\nThe function is built by interpolating constant height onto the grid points between start_i and end_i. The integral constraint is maintained through a repair mechanism. The objective is evaluated by computing the maximum correlation between the function and its time-shifted complement.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Setup:\n      - n_points grid points from 0 to 2\n      - Determine n_half = n_points//2\n      - dx = 2.0/n_points, integral_target = 280/2 for the parent case (adjust if n_points changes)\n\n   b. Candidate representation:\n      - n_segments parameter (2-6)\n      - Vector of boundaries [start_0, start_1, ..., start_{n_segments-1}] (non-decreasing, must be < n_points)\n\n   c. Objective evaluation:\n      - Build the full height functions of length n_points from the interval boundaries and heights\n      - Compute correlation with 1-full function in mode='full'\n      - Find maximum and return max*dx (scaled by grid spacing)\n\n   d. Feasibility check:\n      - Clip values to [0,1]\n      - Scale heights to meet integral_target when necessary\n      - Ensure non-decreasing boundaries\n\n   e. Optimization:\n      - Use OptimizeBayes TPE algorithm with time budget tracking\n      - Adaptive exploration: start with n_segments=2-3 then increase to 4-6\n      - Repair invalid candidates by scaling while preserving breakpoints\n      - Minimize the correlation maximum\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Start with n_segments=3 (the parent had 1)\n   - Progressively increase n_segments as score improves\n   - Each segment has fixed duration (width)\n   - Breakpoints must start from 0 and be increasing, and end at most n_points-1\n   - Default duration: min(20, n_points//n_segments // 2)\n   - Prioritize candidates closer to time limit\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - TPE estimates dominant cost: ~10-20 evaluations for each n_segments level\n   - Evaluation cost O(n_points^2) due to correlation calculation (approx 70000 operations per eval)\n   - Time estimate: 50-70 seconds for n_points=280 and 1 CPU\n   - Early termination check at mid point (50% budget)\n   - Live cycle: select most promising candidate, evaluate, update algorithm\n   - Fall back: if optimization terminates, return best known candidate\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Feasibility checks: \n     - 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n     - sum(h_full) = integral_target (within tolerance)\n   - Calculate correlation with clipping overrides\n   - Return best candidate found (any representation) without transformation\n   - Support candidate averaging for reduced noise\n   - Time budget: 100 seconds total\n   - Penalties: scale by high factor if beyond time limit\n\n   The candidates are represented as:\n   ```\n   def make_candidate(boundaries):\n       n_segments = len(boundaries)\n       # Validate boundaries here\n       full_h = np.zeros(n_points)\n       for seg_start in boundaries:\n           x_indices = ... # find grid indices >= seg_start\n   ```\n   Evaluation ensures grid coverage and symmetry.",
